course: dictionary
lesson: 6631
title: The word nous
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Nous is a word with two different lives. In everyday British, Irish and Australian talk, nous means common sense: knowing what to do in real life. «He had the nous to call for help» means he was sensible and quick to work out what to do. Say it like «nowss», to rhyme with «house».
explain: Some words look the same in two languages, and nous is one of them. In French, «nous» is a pronoun meaning «we» or «us», and it sounds like «noo». Context tells you which one you are reading: a sentence about a clever plan is English, and a sentence with «nous sommes» is French.
explain: Long ago, Greek thinkers used nous for the mind or reason, the part of you that thinks clearly. You do not need that meaning for everyday reading, but it explains why a philosopher might use the word.
fact: «Nous» means common sense, or practical cleverness, in informal British, Irish and Australian English.
fact: «Nous» is also the French word for «we» or «us», and it sounds like «noo».
fact: «Nous sommes» means «we are», and «nous avons» means «we have».
fact: In old Greek thinking, nous meant the mind or reason.
fact: Common phrases with nous are «a bit of nous», «business nous» and «the nous to…».
exercise: «He had the nous to call for help.» What does nous mean here? | A large, heavy stone | A type of French bread | Being very tired | *Common sense and quick thinking
exercise: What does «nous sommes contents» mean? | You are tired | *We are happy | They are lost
exercise: Which word is the French for «we» or «us», and sounds like «noo»? | vous | ils | *nous | elle
test: Ella says her dad has business nous. What does she mean? | *He is good at practical sense and knowing what to do | He speaks only French at work | He sells nuts for a living
test: In «Nous sommes mardi», what does nous mean? | Day | You | They | *We
exam: Which statement about nous is true? | It is a Greek word for food only | It rhymes with «tone» in English | *It means common sense in informal British and Australian English, and it is also the French for «we» | It means the number three in French
